    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	## Introduction
    28	
    29	Redblue is a general-purpose programming language designed for readability. Its syntax is based on natural English sentences, making code self-documenting and accessible to beginners while remaining powerful enough for professional software development.
    30	
    31	### Design Goals
    32	
    33	1. **Readability**: Code should be understandable by reading it aloud
    34	2. **Simplicity**: Minimal keywords, no symbolic clutter
    35	3. **Power**: Full support for modern programming paradigms
    36	4. **Performance**: Comparable to Python for typical workloads
    37	
    38	### Hello World
    39	
    40	```redblue
    41	say "Hello, World!"
    42	```
    43	
    44	---
    45	
    46	## Lexical Structure
    47	
    48	### Identifiers
    49	
    50	Identifiers name variables, functions, and objects.
    51	
    52	```
    53	identifier = letter { letter | digit | '_' }
    54	letter = 'a'..'z' | 'A'..'Z' | unicode_letter
    55	```
    56	
    57	**Valid identifiers:**
    58	```redblue
    59	set name to "Alice"
    60	set _private to 10
    61	set camelCase to 20
    62	set 名前 to "Japanese"  // Unicode allowed
    63	```
    64	
    65	### Keywords
    66	
    67	Redblue has 32 keywords:
    68	
    69	| Category | Keywords |
    70	|----------|----------|
    71	| Variables | set, to, is, are, nothing |
    72	| Control | if, then, else, end, when, unless |
    73	| Loops | for, each, in, from, times, while, repeat, until |
    74	| Jumps | break, skip, return, give back |
    75	| Functions | to, takes, needs, might fail |
    76	| Types | number, text, yes/no, list, record, object |
    77	| Modules | module, import, export, as |
    78	| Logic | and, or, not |
    79	| Errors | try, catch, finally, error |
    80	| Objects | has, can, this, that, new, extends |
    81	
    82	### Literals
    83	
    84	```redblue
    85	// Numbers
    86	set n to 42
    87	set f to 3.14
    88	set neg to -10
    89	
    90	// Text (Strings)
    91	set s to "Hello, World!"
    92	set multi to "Line 1
    93	Line 2"
    94	
    95	// Boolean
    96	set flag to yes
    97	set flag to no
    98	
    99	// Nothing
   100	set empty to nothing
   101	
   102	// Lists
   103	set arr to [1, 2, 3]
   104	set words to ["hello", "world"]
   105	
   106	// Records
   107	set person to record {
   108	    name: "Alice",
   109	    age: 30
   110	}
   111	```
   112	
   113	### Comments
   114	
   115	```redblue
   116	// Single line comment
   117	
   118	// This is a multi-line
   119	// comment spanning
   120	// multiple lines
   121	```
   122	
   123	### Whitespace
   124	
   125	Whitespace is ignored except as a statement separator. Use newlines or `end` to close blocks.
   126	
   127	---
   128	
   129	## Types
   130	
   131	### Primitive Types
   132	
   133	| Type | Description | Example |
   134	|------|-------------|---------|
   135	| `number` | Integers and decimals | `42`, `3.14`, `-10` |
   136	| `text` | Unicode strings | `"Hello"` |
   137	| `yes/no` | Boolean values | `yes`, `no` |
   138	| `nothing` | Null/void | `nothing` |
   139	
   140	### Complex Types
   141	
   142	```redblue
   143	// List of a type
   144	set numbers to list of number
   145	set names to list of text
   146	
   147	// Record type
   148	set Point to record { x: number, y: number }
   149	
   150	// Object type
   151	set Person to object
   152	```
   153	
   154	### Type Inference
   155	
   156	Redblue infers types automatically:
   157	
   158	```redblue
   159	set x to 10        // x is number
   160	set name to "Hi"   // name is text
   161	```
   162	
   163	### Explicit Types
   164	
   165	```redblue
   166	set x to 10 as number
   167	set name to "Hi" as text
   168	```
   169	
   170	---
   171	
   172	## Variables and Values
   173	
   174	### Declaration
   175	
   176	```redblue
   177	set name to value
   178	set x to 1
   179	set greeting to "Hello"
   180	```
   181	
   182	### Assignment
   183	
   184	```redblue
   185	set name to new_value
   186	```
   187	
   188	### Constants
   189	
   190	```redblue
   191	constant PI to 3.14159
   192	constant MAX_SIZE to 1000
   193	```
   194	
   195	---
   196	
   197	## Expressions
   198	
   199	### Arithmetic
   200	
   201	```redblue
   202	set sum to 1 + 2
   203	set diff to 5 - 3
   204	set product to 4 * 3
   205	set quotient to 10 / 2
   206	set remainder to 10 mod 3
   207	```
   208	
   209	#### Numeric semantics
   210	
   211	A `number` is a 64-bit float. Every operation on numbers is **total**: it either
   212	returns a number or fails with a runtime error. There is no third outcome in
   213	which an operation returns something that is not a number.
   214	
   215	| Expression | Outcome |
   216	|---|---|
   217	| `1 / 0`, `0 / 0`, `-1 / 0` | runtime error: `Division by zero` |
   218	| `5 % 0`, `5 % 0.0`, `5 % -0.0` | runtime error: `Modulo by zero` |
   219	| `-0.0` | the number `0`: negative zero is not a distinct value |
   220	| `1e308 * 1e308` | runtime error: `infinity is not a finite number` |
   221	| `1e400` (a literal too large to hold) | runtime error: `infinity is not a finite number` |
   222	| `1e-400` (a literal too small to hold) | the number `0`: see "Underflow", below |
   223	| `1.2.3`, `2..3`, `3e` (not a number) | lex error: `Invalid number '…'` |
   224	| `-5 % 3` | `-2`: a remainder takes the sign of the dividend |
   225	| `5-2` | `3`: a sign belongs to a literal only after its exponent |
   226	
   227	Three consequences are worth stating plainly:
   228	
   229	1. **A number is always finite.** `NaN`, `infinity` and `-infinity` cannot
   230	   enter a `number`, from arithmetic, from a literal, from `json.parse`, or from
   231	   a library function. There is no way to obtain one, so there is no value whose
   232	   comparisons are all false.
   233	2. **Whole numbers are exact only to 2^53.** `9007199254740993` reads as
   234	   `9007199254740992`. A whole number wider than 2^53 is printed in full rather
   235	   than through a 64-bit integer, so it never reports a number the program does
   236	   not hold.
   237	3. **Non-finite numbers still print.** A number that is not finite can only be
   238	   built by a host program embedding Redblue, and it prints as `not a number`,
   239	   `infinity` or `negative infinity`. `json.stringify` of one is `null`, which is
   240	   what a JSON writer emits for it.
   241	
   242	#### Literals
   243	
   244	A number literal is digits, at most one `.`, then at most one exponent: an `e`,
   245	an optional sign, and digits. Anything else is a mistake in the source and is
   246	reported as a lex error naming the literal — never read as `0`.
   247	
   248	```
   249	5-2        // 5 minus 2, because the sign is not after an exponent
   250	5 + 2      // the same, written with spaces
   251	1e-3       // 0.001: here the sign is after the exponent, so it is part of it
   252	2e-3       // 0.002
   253	1.5e+2     // 150
   254	.5         // 0.5
   255	5.         // 5
   256	1.2.3      // lex error: Invalid number '1.2.3'
   257	```
   258	
   259	#### Underflow
   260	
   261	A literal too small to hold is **not** an error, because there is nothing
   262	outside the reals below it to report: `1e-400` is the number `0`. Overflow is
   263	different — the answer lies outside the reals and the operation fails rather
   264	than naming a number the program does not hold. A `0` produced by underflow is
   265	an ordinary `0` in every other respect, so it is an ordinary zero divisor:
   266	`1 / 1e-400` is `Division by zero`.
   267	
   268	### Comparison
   269	
   270	```redblue
   271	// Text comparison
   272	if name is "Alice"
   273	if x is greater than 10
   274	if x is less than or equal to 100
   275	
   276	// Alternative operators
   277	if x > 10          // is greater than
   278	if x >= 10         // is greater than or equal to
   279	if x < 10          // is less than
   280	if x <= 10         // is less than or equal to
   281	if x == 10         // is equal to
   282	if x != 10         // is not
   283	```
   284	
   285	### Logical
   286	
   287	```redblue
   288	if x is greater than 0 and x is less than 100
   289	if status is "active" or status is "pending"
   290	if not is_empty
   291	```
   292	
   293	### String Interpolation
   294	
   295	```redblue
   296	set greeting to "Hello, {name}!"
   297	set message to "Value: {x + y}"
   298	```
   299	
   300	### Property Access
   301	
   302	```redblue
   303	set first to person.name
   304	set x_coord to point.x
   305	```
   306	
   307	### Function Call
   308	
   309	```redblue
   310	set len to text.length("hello")
   311	set result to math.sqrt(2)
   312	```
   313	
   314	### List Operations
   315	
   316	```redblue
   317	// Indexing
   318	set first to items at 0
   319	set last to items at -1
   320	
   321	// Length
   322	set count to length of items
   323	
   324	// Contains
   325	if "hello" is in words
   326	```
   327	
   328	---
   329	
   330	## Statements
   331	
   332	### If-Then-Else
   333	
   334	```redblue
   335	if condition
   336	    // statements
   337	end
   338	
   339	if condition
   340	    // then branch
   341	else
   342	    // else branch
   343	end
   344	
   345	if x > 0
   346	    say "positive"
   347	else if x < 0
   348	    say "negative"
   349	else
   350	    say "zero"
   351	end
   352	```
   353	
   354	### When (Pattern Matching)
   355	
   356	```redblue
   357	when value
   358	    case 1 then say "one"
   359	    case 2 then say "two"
   360	    else say "other"
   361	end
   362	```
   363	
   364	### Unless
   365	
   366	```redblue
   367	unless is_valid
   368	    say "Invalid!"
   369	end
   370	```
   371	
   372	### For Loop (Iteration)
   373	
   374	```redblue
   375	for each item in items
   376	    say item
   377	end
   378	```
   379	
   380	### For Loop (Range)
   381	
   382	```redblue
   383	for each i from 1 to 10
   384	    say i
   385	end
   386	
   387	// With step
   388	for each i from 0 to 100 by 5
   389	    say i
   390	end
   391	```
   392	
   393	### Repeat Loop
   394	
