   534	
   535	### Constructor
   536	
   537	```redblue
   538	object Person
   539	    has name
   540	    has age
   541	    
   542	    to create(name, age)
   543	        set this.name to name
   544	        set this.age to age
   545	        give back this
   546	    end
   547	end
   548	
   549	set person to new Person("Alice", 30)
   550	```
   551	
   552	### Inheritance
   553	
   554	```redblue
   555	object Employee extends Person
   556	    has salary
   557	    has department
   558	    
   559	    to get_bonus()
   560	        give back this.salary * 0.1
   561	    end
   562	end
   563	```
   564	
   565	### Lookup order and shadowing
   566	
   567	A declaration is both the type and its prototype: `object Person ... end` binds
   568	the name `Person` to a record of the fields the declaration resolves to, so
   569	`type_of(Person)` is `"record"` and `Person` is the value a method sees as
   570	`this`.
   571	
   572	Lookup order, for both fields and methods, is **nearest declaration first**: the
   573	child's own `has` fields and `to can` methods, then the parent's, then the
   574	grandparent's, and so on. The first declaration of a name in that order wins
   575	and the further ones are not copied in, so a child field or method **shadows**
   576	its parent's rather than merging with it. `Employee.role()` is the child's
   577	`role`, and `Person.role()` is still the parent's. A field and a method of one
   578	name are separate: `Employee.name` is the field, `Employee.name()` is the
   579	method.
   580	
   581	Four rules follow from that order:
   582	
   583	| Rule | Why |
   584	|---|---|
   585	| The parent must be declared before the child | The chain is walked once, at declaration. An undeclared parent is an error, not an empty base. |
   586	| The chain is acyclic by construction | `object A extends A` is a one-object cycle and is reported as one; a two-object cycle cannot be written, because `A` is already declared when `B` is and re-declaring a name is refused. |
   587	| `has` twice keeps the later default | One field, one default, in the position of the first mention. |
   588	| A write is a write to that prototype | `set Person.name to "Ada"` changes the record `Person` names. A child declared afterwards still starts from the *declared* defaults, not from those writes. |
   589	
   590	A method writes to the object it was called on through `this`
   591	(`set this.name to name`) and hands the result back with `give back this`, which
   592	is the constructor SPEC.md § Constructor is written as. The write does not
   593	escape to the declaration by itself: a record is a value, so a method that
   594	mutates `this` and returns nothing leaves the prototype as it was.
   595	
   596	A call is a method call when the receiver names a declared object, and a module
   597	function (`receiver_function`, as `files.read` is) when it does not. `this` is
   598	bound by a method call and by nothing else, so reaching for it in free code is
   599	an error rather than an empty record. A method on a receiver that names no
   600	object is not guessed at: a missing method and a non-object receiver are each
   601	their own error.
   602	
   603	### Properties
   604	
   605	```redblue
   606	object Circle
   607	    has radius
   608	    has color default "white"
   609	    
   610	    to area()
   611	        give back math.PI * this.radius * this.radius
   612	    end
   613	end
   614	```
