  "finding": "src/vm.rs:517-523 — `Statement::Break` and `Statement::Skip` each carry `// TODO: Implement proper control flow` and evaluate to `Ok(Value::Nothing)`. No loop consults them: `for each` (src/vm.rs:~460), `repeat` (src/vm.rs:489) and `while` (src/vm.rs:505) all run their body to completion. Verified against the built binary: `for each i in [1,2,3] / if i is 2 then break end / say i / end` prints 1 2 3 and exits 0, and the same program with `break` after `set n to n + 1` in `repeat 5 times` still reports n = 5. SPEC.md:417-433 documents both statements with worked examples (`if i is 50 / break / end`), GRAMMAR.md:245-246 lists them, and src/lexer.rs:19-20 reserves the words and src/parser.rs:433-441 parses them — so the language accepts a program that compiles, runs, exits 0 and gives the WRONG ANSWER. Worse, two green tests pin the broken behaviour as correct: tests/test_lists.rb:108 `test \"lists: break inside a loop does not truncate it\"` asserts `expect total to be 10` when a working `break` at value 3 would make it 3, and tests/test_lists.rb:92 `test \"lists: a conditional inside a loop does not disturb the iteration\"` asserts `expect seen to be 4` when a working `skip` at value 2 would make it 3. A silent no-op that returns success is the most expensive class of defect this pipeline can ship.",
